What Is Multi-Location Review Management?
Multi-location review management is the coordinated process of generating, monitoring, analyzing, responding to, and acting on customer reviews across a portfolio of locations. It gives the organization one operating system for local reputation while preserving the context and judgment that belong at each location.

The work becomes materially harder as a business adds locations. Each location has its own review profile, customers, employees, competitors, service patterns, and operating realities. The brand still needs consistent standards, approved corporate and brand knowledge that can be combined with accurate local facts, reporting topics that match the business, comparable measures, and a clear view of risk. A successful program connects those two levels.

This is why aggregate reporting can be misleading. Birdeye reported that, in an analysis of 1,762 multi-location brands, individual locations were roughly three times more visible in AI answers than the brand-level score suggested. It also found that 46.7% of brands had a visibility gap of at least 50 points between their best and worst locations. That is Birdeye's research, and the practical lesson is broader: customers experience and evaluate the location in front of them, not an average location.

BrightLocal's 2026 Local Consumer Review Survey found that 97% of consumers read reviews for local businesses, 74% focus on reviews written in the previous three months, and 31% will only use a business with at least a 4.5-star rating. The survey also found that consumers evaluate the business response itself. For a multi-location organization, uneven review recency, rating, and response quality can create very different customer experiences under the same brand.

Multi-location review management operating model connecting corporate standards, regional oversight, and local action
The operating model works in both directions. Standards, corporate and brand messages, and approved knowledge move outward. Local facts, customer evidence, and emerging topics move back to the organization.
The five jobs a multi-location program must perform
Generate authentic reviews: Give each location a repeatable, low-friction way to ask customers for feedback.
Monitor the full portfolio: See new reviews, unanswered reviews, unusual activity, and location-level performance without opening dozens of profiles.
Respond with quality and control: Use the reviewer's experience, relevant business knowledge, brand rules, location context, and human judgment where needed.
Understand customers: Turn review text into comparable themes, sentiment, supporting language, and assigned operating work.
Understand competitors: Compare each location with the businesses customers actually see nearby, including ratings, volume, recency, themes, responses, and local visibility.
Why Multi-Location Review Programs Break
Most failures are management failures before they are software failures. The organization has not decided which work belongs centrally, which work belongs locally, what can be automated, when a person must become involved, or how a customer insight becomes an operating action. The tool then reproduces that ambiguity at scale.

The common sources of failure
One portfolio average hides weak locations. A regional rating can look healthy while an individual location is losing trust, reviews, or visibility.
Corporate teams lack local context. They can enforce standards, yet may not know the employee involved, the local process, or the recovery path.
Local teams lack time or confidence. Managers know what happened but often handle review work between other responsibilities.
Every response sounds the same. Templates and generic AI produce coverage while adding little useful information for the reviewer or the next customer.
Review analysis stops at a dashboard. Themes are displayed but nobody validates them, assigns an owner, changes the operation, or measures recurrence.
The program ignores the local competitive set. A location may improve internally while nearby competitors improve faster.
Who Owns What? A Practical Governance Model
The right operating model is rarely fully centralized or fully local. Corporate teams should own the standards and infrastructure that benefit from consistency. Location teams should own the context, customer recovery, and operating decisions that require proximity to the work. Regional leaders connect the two.

Governance responsibilities and decision rights by organizational level
Level	Primary responsibilities	Decisions that should stay here
Corporate or franchisor	Brand rules, approved knowledge, platform configuration, risk standards, measurement definitions, enterprise reporting, vendor management	What the brand may say, what the system may automate, which exceptions require escalation, and how performance is measured
Regional or portfolio leader	Performance review, outlier detection, coaching, resource allocation, escalation support, cross-location learning	Which locations need intervention, which practices should be replicated, and which problems require central support
Location, franchisee, or multi-unit operator	Customer context, review requests at the point of work, local facts, customer recovery, employee coaching, operating action	What happened locally, who should contact the customer, and what should change in the operation
Shared or escalated	Legal, compliance, safety, discrimination, threats, privacy, suspected fraud, high-value customer issues, media risk	Who has authority to respond publicly, what evidence is preserved, and which parallel recovery or dispute process should begin
Franchise systems require an additional distinction. The franchisor owns the brand and system standards. The franchisee owns the customer relationship and local operation. A franchisee group with one location has different capacity from a group with twenty, so permissions and reporting should accommodate both without treating every franchisee as a single store manager.

For Google Business Profile administration, Google supports bulk management and verification for eligible organizations with 10 or more locations. That solves part of the profile-management problem. The organization still needs its own decision rights for requests, responses, recovery, analysis, and follow-through.

