Choose the request method by operating context
Triggered email or SMS
Useful when a CRM, POS, scheduling, property, or practice system can mark the interaction complete.
Manager or employee send
Useful when the person closest to the customer knows the right moment and can enter the customer while the interaction is fresh.
QR code or durable link
Useful for receipts, leave-behinds, signage, events, and employee-specific attribution.
Campaign or milestone request
Useful for repeat relationships such as residents, patients, members, customers, and clients who have natural moments worth reviewing.
Measure the full request funnel
Review count alone does not explain whether the process works. Track eligible customers, requests sent, deliveries, opens or clicks where available, completed reviews, private feedback, time to review, rating, review text rate, and conversion by location, employee, channel, and campaign. These measures show whether a location has a customer-experience problem, an asking problem, or an execution problem.

Protect authenticity while growing volume
Google's Maps policy allows businesses to solicit genuine reviews without incentives or attempts to influence the rating or content. The FTC's Consumer Reviews and Testimonials Rule prohibits incentives conditioned on a particular positive or negative sentiment. A scalable program should therefore preserve neutral request language, customer choice, traceable campaign settings, and clear vendor practices across every location.

RRAI operating principle: A better request does not write the review. It uses real context to help the customer remember the experience and removes friction from sharing it.

