2. Respond With Central Knowledge and Local Judgment
A public review begins with the reviewer, but the response has more than one audience. It communicates back to the reviewer, helps future customers decide whether to choose the business, and creates a visible record for employees and leaders. A useful response can acknowledge, clarify, recover, explain, differentiate, or direct the next step.

Coverage is the starting point. Contribution is the goal.
A generic response may satisfy a response-rate metric while contributing very little. The response becomes valuable when it understands what the customer said, chooses relevant approved business knowledge, follows company and location rules, and recognizes when a person should take over. This is especially important across many locations, where the same brand can offer different amenities, service paths, teams, or recovery instructions. A buyer should ask whether the platform has a real knowledge layer or only a tone selector and a general prompt.

Use a multi-step response process
Understand the review. Identify the experience, names, themes, sentiment, questions, rating-text mismatches, updates, and sensitive details.
Check risk and routing. Detect privacy concerns, malicious instructions, threats, discrimination, safety issues, unresolved complaints, or other reasons for human attention.
Select relevant knowledge. Choose only the approved corporate, brand, and location facts or marketing messages that genuinely fit the review. The system should know which level owns each message and when it may be used.
Plan the response. Decide what the response should accomplish, what it should say, what it should avoid, and whether the conversation should continue privately.
Write and review. Produce natural language from the plan, remove repetition, confirm the facts, and route the response to the chosen approval or publishing workflow.
