How to Choose Review Management Software for Multiple Locations
The best platform depends on the scope of the job. Some organizations need a broad local-marketing suite that includes listings, pages, social, and reviews. Others need deeper review generation, a managed knowledge layer, response quality, customizable customer-intelligence topics, competitive review analysis, or local rank tracking. Define the operating system first, then decide whether one platform or a connected stack best fits it.

Use this capability checklist
Portfolio structure: Company, brand, region, franchisee group, and location hierarchy with rollups that match the real organization.
Roles and permissions: Clear control over who can view, generate, edit, approve, publish, change rules, and see customer data.
Review request depth: Correct-location routing, email/SMS/QR, customer and service context, approved corporate, brand, and local messages, optional photos and neutral questions, follow-ups, employee attribution, and funnel reporting.
Knowledge architecture and response quality: Managed corporate, brand, and location libraries; review understanding; relevance selection; response planning; specialized checks; brand rules; and useful finished responses. Ask who owns each knowledge level, how messages are updated, and whether the platform can show what it selected.
Human escalation: Routing by risk and consequence, mobile approval, customer-recovery ownership, notes, status, and a clear record of the final action.
Customer intelligence: A customizable category and topic taxonomy, phrase-level sentiment analysis, source-review validation, location comparisons, outlier alerts, assignment, and recurrence measurement.
Competitive intelligence: Local competitor discovery, rating and review momentum, response behavior, comparable themes, and map rank tracking.
Integrations and ease of use: A practical way to add customers, act from a phone, connect existing systems, export data, and support both low-capacity and sophisticated teams.
Pricing at scale: Transparent per-location and usage assumptions, plan-specific feature access, included limits, add-ons, contract terms, and pooled volume where appropriate.
Ask vendors to demonstrate the hard parts
Show how a request changes when the customer, location, service, and available context change. Ask which approved corporate, brand, or local knowledge was selected and why. Then show the conversion reporting behind those requests.
Use one positive, one negative, one ambiguous, and one sensitive review. Ask the vendor to show what the system understood, which corporate, brand, or local knowledge it selected, where that knowledge is maintained, what it avoided, and why a person became involved.
Ask the vendor to create or modify a reporting category and topic, then show how source reviews are classified. Move from a portfolio metric to the underlying location and source reviews, assign an action, and show how recurrence is measured.
Compare a location with nearby competitors. Look at rating, volume, recency, response behavior, customer themes, and map visibility.
Model the real organization. Test a corporate user, regional user, 20-location franchisee group, single-location franchisee, and local manager.
Calculate the bill at current and future scale. Include locations, request volume, review volume, SMS, analysis, map scans, add-ons, support, and implementation.
