Common Multi-Location Mistakes
Managing the average location
Portfolio averages help executives orient themselves, but they do not reveal what a customer sees at a specific location. Always preserve the location view and the evidence behind it.

Centralizing every response
Corporate control can improve consistency, yet a corporate team may lack the local facts needed for recovery or a useful reply. Centralize standards and knowledge. Route context and decisions to the people who have them.

Giving every location unrestricted control
Local autonomy without common rules creates uneven voice, missed reviews, risky responses, and data that cannot be compared. Give local teams clear authority inside a defined operating system.

Using templates as the quality standard
Templates can help a team respond, but repetition becomes visible across a multi-location portfolio. Evaluate whether responses acknowledge the actual experience and add relevant knowledge selected from maintained corporate, brand, and location sources.

Letting the knowledge library go stale
Knowledge is operating content. A platform can select relevant knowledge only when corporate messages, brand positioning, local services, amenities, policies, people, and recovery instructions remain current. Assign an owner and review cadence to each knowledge level.

Treating insights as finished work
A chart is a finding. It becomes management when the pattern is validated, assigned, acted on, and measured.

Comparing every location with the same target
A location competes in its own market. Use internal benchmarks for operational discipline and local competitors for commercial reality.

Frequently Asked Questions
What is the best way to manage reviews across multiple locations?
Use one system with a verified location inventory, shared standards, location-specific knowledge, clear roles, a unified inbox, review-request workflows, response approvals, topic-level analysis, competitive benchmarks, and location-level reporting. Keep customer recovery and operating decisions close to the location while corporate owns the common rules and infrastructure.

Should corporate or local managers respond to reviews?
Most organizations benefit from a hybrid model. Corporate owns brand rules, approved knowledge, platform configuration, and escalation standards. Local teams contribute context, manage customer recovery, and handle operating actions. Automation can complete routine work, while consequential reviews route to an authorized person.

How can a franchise manage reviews across all locations?
Model the franchisor, franchisee group, individual franchisee, and location as distinct roles. Give the franchisor visibility and brand controls, then give franchisees and local teams the permissions and context needed to act. Use shared measures so performance is comparable without pretending every franchisee has the same capacity.

How can we get more Google reviews at every location?
Create a repeatable request path tied to an appropriate customer moment, route every customer to the correct profile, use email, SMS, QR, or employee-specific links, reduce the steps required to respond, and measure the request funnel by location. Relevant customer and service context can make the request easier to recognize and complete.

Can review requests be personalized without influencing the customer?
Yes. A request can reference real context such as the location, service, date, employee, product, milestone, or completed-work photo. Neutral questions can help the customer recall what happened. The customer still chooses whether to review and what opinion to express.

How quickly should each location respond to reviews?
Set a service level that the organization can consistently meet, then make sensitive reviews faster. Consumer expectations vary, but BrightLocal's 2026 research shows that response speed and response quality both affect trust. Automation should reduce delay while preserving review understanding, relevant knowledge, and human escalation.

How do we compare review performance across locations?
Use rating, review volume, velocity, recency, response coverage, response time, request conversion, and topic-level sentiment. Preserve the underlying customer phrases so leaders can validate what is driving the difference. Compare each location with both internal peers and its actual local competitors.

How do we handle fake, spam, or unfair reviews across many locations?
Create an incident workflow to detect unusual activity, validate the review against available records, preserve links and screenshots, assign an owner, separate the public response from customer recovery and the platform dispute, and track the outcome. Google provides a reporting and appeal process for inappropriate content. A strong program makes that process repeatable across the portfolio.

What should multi-location review management software include?
Look for portfolio hierarchy, roles and permissions, location-specific review requests, managed corporate, brand, and location knowledge, response quality and approvals, mobile action, customer-recovery routing, customizable topic and sentiment analysis, competitive review intelligence, map rank tracking, integrations, exports, and pricing that remains clear as location and usage volume grow.

What knowledge should a multi-location review platform use?
The knowledge should mirror the organization. Corporate-level content may cover company facts, shared policies, approved claims, and key marketing messages. Brand-level content can capture positioning, offerings, and voice. Location-level content should cover actual services, amenities, people, hours, local differentiators, and recovery instructions. The important capability is relevance selection. The platform should use only the knowledge that fits the review or request and show the team where that knowledge came from.

